v1.4.0 — security hardening: sessions, requests, stored data, sweeps

Applies the remediation plan written from the third-party audit (44 findings
put through an 89-agent verification pass, which judged 36 of the audit's own
proposed fixes unsound and refuted two findings outright). Those two refuted
claims are deliberately not acted on: removing /static/app.css from
PUBLIC_PATHS (it breaks the sign-in page) and the X-Forwarded-For finding
(gated behind an off-by-default TRUST_PROXY).

Sessions
- Signing out rotates session_epoch, so it revokes every token for that user on
  every device rather than only dropping this browser's cookie.
- A sign-in waits up to 1s for one of the eight concurrency slots instead of
  being refused the instant none is free. The throttle delay keeps its slot on
  purpose: that is what bounds how many guesses a flood lands per second.
- The username is bounded at 64 characters where it is used, not by a model cap
  that could reject an account that already exists.
- Failed sign-ins are logged on the 1st and every 10th attempt per client, and
  every event message is bounded and whitespace-collapsed, so an anonymous
  caller can no longer evict the activity feed.

Requests
- Origin-versus-Host check on every request, before the session check. The VNC
  handshake checks origin before the cookie and answers both with close(1008),
  so it cannot be used to tell the two apart.
- The gate compares request.scope["path"], closing a %3F-in-the-path route past
  the PUBLIC_PATHS check and the Host-header variant of it.
- Four response headers: nosniff, no-referrer, X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN, and
  no-store on /api/. No CSP — the useful one blanks every book cover.
- Range is dropped on the static mount, so starlette's pre-1.6.0 range merge is
  not reachable through the publicly-fetchable assets.

Stored data
- umask 077 for the whole process, plus a one-off chmod at startup for what is
  already on disk. The media library is deliberately excluded, and both places
  that create directories under it restore a traversable mode.
- GOODREADS_SECRET_KEY is refused at first use below 32 characters, with a
  message of its own so it is not confused with the empty case.
- forget-session deletes the stored Goodreads session and the browser profile,
  and refuses while a browser is running — checked against the process table,
  because the CLI is a different process from the server that owns it.

Talking to your services
- Redirects are refused rather than followed, and reported as a misconfigured
  service rather than as an outage, so the breaker cannot hold a permanent
  misconfiguration for 24 hours.
- Response bodies are capped at 16 MiB and refused whole rather than truncated,
  with the transfer headers dropped on the rebuild: decoded bytes must not be
  decoded twice, or a gzipped upstream reports as an outage and is forgiven.
- Error bodies are withheld from any client that authenticates, so a credential
  cannot reach stage_runs.detail. ClientError.body exposes the text to the one
  caller that has to branch on it (BookLore answers 400 both to a live-token
  conflict and to a wrong password, so the status cannot stand in for it).
- The Test button runs the same authenticated probe as the health check, so a
  wrong API key fails it instead of passing an unauthenticated route.
- An epub's two XML documents are size-capped and entity definitions refused.

Sweeps
- The 120s budget now covers the periodic phases, and the worker pool outlives
  the sweep instead of being joined by ThreadPoolExecutor.__exit__ — which is
  what made the budget advisory. Measured live, sweeps against that budget were
  ending at 310-432s.
- A book left mid-advance is tracked in _in_flight so it cannot be run twice,
  and a failed submit releases its claim instead of leaking the book forever.

Container
- no-new-privileges, pids_limit: 512 (measured: idle 12, login browser 157),
  and every capability dropped except DAC_OVERRIDE, which /data requires — the
  bind source is owned by the host user's uid, so uid 0 cannot write to it
  without that one.
- fastapi 0.135.0 with an explicit starlette 1.6.0 pin, the oldest starlette
  that bounds how many byte ranges it merges. python-multipart removed: it was
  never used.

Docs
- SECURITY.md rewritten around a "what is enforced, and where" table, so the
  posture can be read in one page before the reasoning.
- PUBLISH_HOST guidance corrected. It is not exposure control — a tunnel in
  front reaches the app whichever address the port is bound to — and narrowing
  it to an address the front end does not dial takes the site down, because a
  proxy in a container dials the host as its bridge gateway, never as
  127.0.0.1. The corrected note names the command that tells you which.

tests/ grows from 25 modules to 35 (348 passing on a dev host; 347 passing with
one root-only skip in the image under Python 3.10). requirements-dev.txt
declares pytest, which nothing did before.
